
---
const title = "How to Calculate Your Small Business Profit in Kenya";
const description = "Learn how to calculate gross profit and net profit with simple Kenyan shilling examples, and understand why sales are not the same as profit.";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    <title>{title} | Biashivo Business Insights</title>
    <style>
      :root {
        font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
        color: #24364b;
        background: #f7fafc;
      }
      * { box-sizing: border-box; }
      body { margin: 0; }
      a { color: #008e74; }
      .container { width: min(1120px, 92%); margin: auto; }
      header { background: #0c2033; padding: 24px 0; }
      .header-inner {
        display: flex;
        justify-content: space-between;
        align-items: center;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        gap: 20px;
      }
      .brand {
        color: white;
        text-decoration: none;
        font-size: 23px;
        font-weight: 800;
        letter-spacing: 1px;
      }
      .brand span { color: #00c9a7; }
      .back-link {
        color: #d3e1e9;
        text-decoration: none;
        font-size: 14px;
      }
      .article {
        width: min(800px, 90%);
        margin: 65px auto 85px;
        background: white;
        padding: clamp(24px, 5vw, 65px);
        border: 1px solid #e2e9ef;
        border-radius: 16px;
      }
      .category {
        color: #008e74;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        font-size: 13px;
        font-weight: bold;
        letter-spacing: 1.5px;
      }
      h1 {
        color: #0c2033;
        font-size: clamp(32px, 5vw, 48px);
        line-height: 1.18;
        margin: 18px 0 24px;
      }
      .intro {
        color: #43566a;
        font-size: 19px;
        line-height: 1.85;
        margin-bottom: 38px;
      }
      h2 {
        color: #0c2033;
        font-size: 27px;
        margin: 42px 0 15px;
        line-height: 1.35;
      }
      p, li { font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.9; }
      ul { padding-left: 24px; }
      li { margin-bottom: 10px; }
      .formula {
        background: #eaf4f3;
        border-left: 4px solid #00a989;
        padding: 20px 24px;
        border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
        margin: 28px 0;
      }
      .formula p { margin: 0; }
      .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 28px 0; }
      table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; }
      th, td {
        border-bottom: 1px solid #e2e9ef;
        padding: 14px;
        text-align: left;
      }
      th { background: #eaf4f3; color: #0c2033; }
      td:last-child, th:last-child { text-align: right; }
      .total { font-weight: bold; color: #0c2033; }
      .closing {
        margin-top: 48px;
        padding-top: 24px;
        border-top: 1px solid #e2e9ef;
      }
      .button {
        display: inline-block;
        margin-top: 20px;
        background: #00a989;
        color: white;
        padding: 14px 22px;
        border-radius: 8px;
        text-decoration: none;
        font-weight: bold;
      }
      footer {
        background: #071726;
        color: #cbd5df;
        text-align: center;
        padding: 32px 16px;
        font-size: 14px;
      }
      @media (max-width: 600px) {
        .article { margin: 25px auto 50px; }
        h2 { font-size: 23px; }
      }
    </style>
  </head>

  <body>
    <header>
      <div class="container header-inner">
        <a class="brand" href="/">BIASHI<span>VO</span></a>
        <a class="back-link" href="/#articles">← Back to Business Insights</a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <main>
      <article class="article">
        <div class="category">Profit Management • Practical Guide</div>
        <h1>How to Calculate Your Small Business Profit in Kenya</h1>

        <p class="intro">
          Your business may be making sales every day, but how much
          money are you actually earning? Understanding profit helps
          you set better prices, control expenses, and plan for growth.
        </p>

        <h2>1. Understand the Difference Between Sales and Profit</h2>
        <p>
          Sales revenue is the money your business earns from selling
          products or services before deducting costs.
        </p>
        <p>
          Profit is what remains after deducting the relevant
          business costs and expenses.
        </p>
        <p>
          For example, if your business sells goods worth KSh 100,000,
          that doesn't mean you've made KSh 100,000 in profit.
        </p>

        <h2>2. Calculate Your Gross Profit</h2>
        <p>
          Gross profit is the amount remaining after subtracting
          the direct cost of the goods or services sold.
        </p>

        <div class="formula">
          <p>
            <strong>Gross Profit = Sales Revenue − Cost of Goods Sold</strong>
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          Imagine you run a small shop in Nairobi. During one month,
          you sell products worth KSh 100,000. The products you sold
          originally cost you KSh 65,000 to purchase.
        </p>

        <div class="formula">
          <p>
            Sales revenue: KSh 100,000<br />
            Cost of goods sold: KSh 65,000<br />
            <strong>Gross profit: KSh 35,000</strong>
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>3. Calculate Your Net Profit</h2>
        <p>
          Gross profit is not the final amount your business earns.
          You still need to deduct operating expenses such as rent,
          electricity, transport, marketing, and other business costs.
        </p>

        <div class="formula">
          <p>
            <strong>Net Profit = Gross Profit − Operating Expenses</strong>
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>4. Practical Kenyan SME Profit Example</h2>
        <p>
          Let's continue with the small shop example and calculate
          its estimated monthly profit.
        </p>

        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Business item</th>
                <th>Amount (KSh)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Total sales</td><td>100,000</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Cost of goods sold</td><td>65,000</td></tr>
              <tr class="total"><td>Gross profit</td><td>35,000</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Shop rent</td><td>10,000</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Electricity</td><td>2,000</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Transport</td><td>3,000</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Marketing</td><td>2,000</td></tr>
              <tr class="total"><td>Total operating expenses</td><td>17,000</td></tr>
              <tr class="total"><td>Estimated net profit</td><td>18,000</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          In this example, the business made KSh 100,000 in sales,
          but its estimated net profit was only KSh 18,000.
          This is before any additional costs or taxes not included
          in the example.
        </p>

        <h2>5. Calculate Your Profit Margin</h2>
        <p>
          Profit margin tells you what percentage of your sales
          remains as profit.
        </p>

        <div class="formula">
          <p>
            <strong>Net Profit Margin = (Net Profit ÷ Sales) × 100</strong>
            <br />
            (18,000 ÷ 100,000) × 100 = <strong>18%</strong>
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          This means the business keeps approximately KSh 18
          in net profit for every KSh 100 in sales, based on
          the costs included in our example.
        </p>

        <h2>6. Common Mistakes When Calculating Profit</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Counting all sales revenue as profit.</li>
          <li>Forgetting small expenses such as delivery and packaging.</li>
          <li>Mixing personal spending with business expenses.</li>
          <li>Ignoring the cost of unsold inventory.</li>
          <li>Confusing customer deposits with final earnings.</li>
          <li>Failing to review profit regularly.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>7. Simple Habits to Improve Your Profit Tracking</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Record every sale and business expense.</li>
          <li>Track the cost of products or services sold.</li>
          <li>Review gross and net profit at least monthly.</li>
          <li>Compare expenses across different months.</li>
          <li>Use consistent records instead of relying on memory.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>How Biashivo Plans to Help</h2>
        <p>
          Biashivo is being developed to help small business owners
          record sales, expenses, customers, and orders, and
          understand their business performance more easily.
        </p>
        <p>
          Our goal is to make everyday business management
          simpler for Kenyan entrepreneurs, without requiring
          complicated accounting knowledge.
        </p>

        <div class="closing">
          <h2>Know your numbers. Grow your business.</h2>
          <p>
            Understanding profit is one of the most important steps
            toward building a sustainable business.
          </p>
          <a class="button" href="/#articles">
            ← Explore More Business Insights
          </a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </main>

    <footer>
      <p>© {new Date().getFullYear()} Biashivo. All rights reserved.</p>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
